1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,927
In today's astronomy lecture,

2
00:00:01,927 --> 00:00:08,477
I'm going to talk about the need for a system to manage the movement of satellites

3
00:00:08,477 --> 00:00:11,560
and other objects in orbit around the Earth.

4
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:15,780
In other words, a Space Traffic Management system.

5
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:25,660
We already have effective Air Traffic Control systems that are used internationally to ensure that planes navigate our skies safely.

6
00:00:25,660 --> 00:00:29,400
Well, Space Traffic Management is a similar concept

7
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:33,300
but focusing on the control of satellites.

8
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:42,490
The aim of such a system would be to prevent the danger of collisions in space between the objects in orbit around the Earth.

9
00:00:42,490 --> 00:00:48,246
In order to do this, we'd need to have a set of legal measures,

10
00:00:48,246 --> 00:00:54,770
and we'd also have to develop the technical systems to enable us to prevent such accidents.

11
00:00:54,770 --> 00:01:02,470
But unfortunately, at present we don't actually have a Space Traffic Management system that works.

12
00:01:02,470 --> 00:01:08,470
So why not? What are the problems in developing such a system?

13
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:12,542
Well, for one thing, satellites are relatively cheap these days,

14
00:01:12,542 --> 00:01:16,060
compared with how they were in the past

15
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:20,280
meaning that more people can afford to put them into space.

16
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:22,960
So there's a lot more of them out there

17
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:27,852
and people aren't just launching single satellites

18
00:01:27,852 --> 00:01:32,900
but whole constellations, consisting of thousands of them designed to work together.

19
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:37,340
So space is getting more crowded every day.

20
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:39,740
But in spite of this

21
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:45,756
one thing you may be surprised to learn is that you can launch a satellite into space

22
00:01:45,756 --> 00:01:47,560
and, once it's out there

23
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:53,790
it doesn't have to send back any information to Earth to allow its identification.

24
00:01:53,790 --> 00:01:58,240
So while we have international systems for ensuring we know

25
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:00,314
where the planes in our skies are,

26
00:02:00,314 --> 00:02:03,380
and to prevent them from colliding with one another

27
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:06,838
when it comes to the safety of satellites,

28
00:02:06,838 --> 00:02:12,210
at present we don't have anything like enough proper ways of tracking them.

29
00:02:12,210 --> 00:02:16,620
And it isn't just entire satellites that we need to consider.

30
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:22,853
A greater threat is the huge amount of space debris in orbit around the Earth -

31
00:02:22,853 --> 00:02:27,840
broken bits of satellite and junk from space stations and so on.

32
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:33,120
And some of these are so small that they can be very hard to identify

33
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:36,460
but they can still be very dangerous.

34
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:44,100
In addition, some operators may be unwilling to share information about the satellites they've launched.

35
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:48,306
For example, a satellite may be designed for military purposes,

36
00:02:48,306 --> 00:02:52,092
or it may have been launched for commercial reasons,

37
00:02:52,092 --> 00:02:57,140
and the operators don't want competitors to have information about it.

38
00:02:57,140 --> 00:03:01,020
And even if the operators are willing to provide it

39
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:04,400
the information isn't easy to collect.

40
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:07,640
Details are needed about the object itself

41
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:11,252
as well as about its location at a particular time -

42
00:03:11,252 --> 00:03:15,160
and remember that a satellite isn't very big

43
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:20,120
and it's likely to be moving at thousands of kilometres an hour.

44
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:27,692
We don't have any sensors that can constantly follow something moving so fast,

45
00:03:27,692 --> 00:03:32,240
so all that the scientists can do is to put forward a prediction concerning

46
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:38,510
where the satellite is heading next.

47
00:03:38,510 --> 00:03:43,320
So those are some of the problems that we're facing.

48
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:48,190
Let's consider now some of the solutions that have been suggested.

49
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:53,480
One key issue is the way in which information is dealt with.

50
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:59,581
We need more information, but it also needs to be accessible at a global level,

51
00:03:59,581 --> 00:04:06,496
so we need to establish shared standards that we can all agree on for the way in

52
00:04:06,496 --> 00:04:08,530
which this information is presented.

53
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:12,220
We already do this in other areas of science

54
00:04:12,560 --> 00:04:17,290
so although this is a challenge, it's not an impossible task.

55
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:20,660
Then, as all this information's collected

56
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:24,434
it needs to be put together so it can be used,

57
00:04:24,434 --> 00:04:29,990
and that will involve creating a single database on which it can be entered.

58
00:04:29,990 --> 00:04:34,000
As we continue to push forward new developments

59
00:04:34,440 --> 00:04:38,440
congestion of the space environment is only going to increase.

60
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:47,866
To cope with this, we need to develop a system like the one I've described to coordinate the work of the numerous spacecraft operators,

61
00:04:47,866 --> 00:04:55,031
but it's also essential that this system is one that establishes trust in the people that use it,

62
00:04:55,031 --> 00:04:57,670
both nationally and at a global level.

63
00:04:57,670 --> 00:05:01,320
One interesting development...
